<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yan (Five Dynasties)</h1><p class="st-meta">911 CE – 913 CE · capital Youzhou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A brief empire proclaimed at Youzhou, near modern Beijing, by the warlord Liu Shouguang, crushed within two years by Jin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-rengong">Liu Rengong</a> was military governor of Lulong, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>, in the late <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a>. In 907 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shouguang">Liu Shouguang</a> imprisoned him and seized the province. In 911, after declining offers from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, Liu Shouguang proclaimed himself emperor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yan-an-shi-rebellion">Great Yan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sources portray <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shouguang">Liu Shouguang</a> as arrogant and cruel, ruling by fear and heavy taxation. The state held the north-eastern frontier, bordering the rising Khitan of the future <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, but had little support among its officials or neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a> sent his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dewei">Zhou Dewei</a> against Yan in 911. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a> fell in 913, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shouguang">Liu Shouguang</a> was captured and executed in 914, and his father was also put to death. Yan's territory became part of Jin and later of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yan-five-dynasties.jpg" alt="Yan (Five Dynasties)" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>911 CE – 913 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Youzhou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liu Shouguang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Yan (Liu Shouguang), Yan (911-913)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=912&amp;focus=yan-five-dynasties" aria-label="Yan (Five Dynasties) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7548 750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yan (Five Dynasties) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7868 1006L7807 1072"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7868 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=912&amp;focus=yan-five-dynasties">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

